1. The main thesis is that suffering and evil are not objective realities but subjective choices—we create our experiences by choosing what and how to perceive the world. Since God creates everything, our subjective experience is a personal perspective, and we can change our experience by changing our beliefs or perspective.

2. I don't fully agree. While the text highlights the role of perception and agency, it risks minimizing real suffering by framing it as merely a choice. People who experience genuine hardship may not have the freedom or ability to simply "choose" to see it differently, and dismissing their pain as a subjective illusion can feel dismissive rather than helpful.

3. In a world of infinite light,
We choose what we see and feel.
Suffering is a lens we wear,
A choice we make, though hard to bear.
Change the view, and the view changes—
A path to peace, though it may sting. [end of text]


